You can also work ninmu/nanmu or fetsi/nakni into the the description=20
itself and not use a relative clause as gleki has done below.

See, the thing is, Lojban isn't like German and French and such. There's=20
no grammatical "gender" in the usual sense.=C2=A0 If you want to say=20
something is male, *say* it's male.=C2=A0 If you want to say it's female,=
=20
*say* it's female.=C2=A0 Same with number.=C2=A0 In the example below, glek=
i's=20
translations say "the *one* small-and-beautiful dancer" (that's what=20
"pa" means.)=C2=A0 Without "pa", it would almost surely be understood the=
=20
same... unless the context made a plural understanding more likely.=C2=A0=
=20
Number isn't marked, nor is gender... Nor is species, even.=C2=A0 If you ne=
ed=20
to specify the dancer is human, you'll have to say so.=C2=A0 (This isn't=20
actually different than English, though.=C2=A0 Anakin Skywalker and Senator=
=20
Palpatine were watching Mon Cala "dancers" while they discussed the=20
health-care benefits of the Dark Side.)

I think you won't find the kind of markers you're looking for in Lojban,=20
unless your range is broader than I'm understanding.

~mark

